Q: Set env variables before running C# integration test in VS Code A: <p>This is supported through an option of <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/tools/dotnet-test-vstest#options" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>dotnet test</code></a></p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong><code>-e|--environment &lt;NAME=&quot;VALUE&quot;&gt;</code></strong></p>
<p>Sets the value of an environment variable. Creates the variable if it doesn't exist, overrides if it does exist. Use of this option will force the tests to be run in an isolated process. The option can be specified multiple times to provide multiple variables.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I tried it for the 3 major test frameworks on .NET 9:</p>
<pre><code>namespace msTestFramework;

[TestClass]
public sealed class Test1
{
    [TestMethod]
    public void TestMethod1()
    {
        Assert.AreEqual(&quot;bar&quot;, Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(&quot;foo&quot;));
    }
}

</code></pre>
<pre><code>
namespace nUnitFramework;

public class Tests
{
    [Test]
    public void Test1()
    {
        Assert.That(Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(&quot;foo&quot;), Is.EqualTo(&quot;bar&quot;));
    }
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>
namespace xUnitFramework;

public class UnitTest1
{
    [Fact]
    public void Test1()
    {
        Assert.Equal(&quot;bar&quot;, Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(&quot;foo&quot;));
    }
}

</code></pre>
<p><code>dotnet test</code> -&gt; all test fail</p>
<p><code>dotnet test -e foo=bar</code> -&gt; all tests pass</p>
